# Emacs configuration review — analysis & refactoring proposal

Date: 2026-07-28
Reviewed: `early-init.el`, `init.el`, `custom.el`, `src/{functions,keymap,vars}.el`
Environment: GNU Emacs 30.2, ~60 packages in `elpa/`, 32 vendored themes in `themes/`
Nothing in the config was modified. This file is notes only.

Method: findings marked **[verified]** were reproduced by loading the config in
batch Emacs and/or byte-compiling it, not just by reading. Commands used are noted
where relevant.

---

## 0. Summary

The config is in good shape overall: `lexical-binding` everywhere, a consistent
`dk/` namespace, `use-package` throughout, a modern completion stack
(vertico + orderless + marginalia + consult + embark + corfu), and built-in
`eglot`/`flymake` rather than heavier alternatives. Comments explain *why* in the
non-obvious places (the vertico-directory DEL rationale, the valign/org-modern
table conflict) — that is better than most personal configs.

Three things are actually broken and silently doing nothing, and there is a set
of duplicated/contradictory settings. Structurally, `init.el` has grown to 547
lines and is the main thing worth refactoring.

Priority order:

| Pri | Item | Section |
|-----|------|---------|
| P0  | Dead eglot server config, dead `C-x C-p`, shadowed consult-projectile keys, `package-selected-packages nil` | §1 |
| P1  | No-op / contradictory settings; tree-sitter installed but never wired up; LSP throughput | §1, §4 |
| P2  | Split `init.el` into modules; move UI suppression into `early-init.el` | §3 |
| P3  | Missing packages (magit, no-littering, apheleia, cape) and free built-ins | §4 |
| P4  | README, Makefile/CI, theme pruning | §5 |

---

## 1. Confirmed bugs

### B1 — All custom eglot server configuration is dead (`init.el:249-256`) — **P0**

```elisp
(add-to-list 'eglot-server-programs
             ''(((haskell-mode haskell-ts-mode) . (...))   ; <-- two quotes
               ...))
```

The `''` means a literal `(quote (...))` list is pushed onto
`eglot-server-programs`. It matches no major mode, so the entry is inert.

**[verified]** After loading the config and `(require 'eglot)`:

```
eglot-server-programs first entry:
  '(((haskell-mode haskell-ts-mode) "haskell-language-server-wrapper" "--lsp") ...)
rust entry lookup: ((rust-ts-mode rust-mode) "rust-analyzer")
```

The rust entry actually in effect is eglot's **built-in** one — so
`rust-analyzer` is running **without** `:check (:command "clippy")`, and clangd
is running **without** `--clang-tidy` and without `-std=c23`. Haskell works only
because eglot ships a default HLS entry too.

Fix — one `add-to-list` per entry, single quote:

```elisp
(with-eval-after-load 'eglot
  (add-to-list 'eglot-server-programs
               '((rust-ts-mode rust-mode)
                 . ("rust-analyzer"
                    :initializationOptions (:check (:command "clippy")))))
  (add-to-list 'eglot-server-programs
               '((c-ts-mode c-mode c++-ts-mode c++-mode)
                 . ("clangd" "--clang-tidy"
                    :initializationOptions (:fallbackFlags ["-std=c23"])))))
```

Note `add-to-list` prepends, and eglot takes the **first** match, so prepending
is correct here.

### B2 — `C-x C-p` is bound to a command that does not exist (`init.el:220`) — **P0**

`consult-project-find` is not defined by consult (only `consult-project-buffer`
exists). `use-package :bind` creates an autoload, so the key *looks* bound.

**[verified]** Invoking it:

```
consult-project-find ERROR: (error "Autoloading file .../consult.elc
  failed to define function consult-project-find")
```

Fix: use `consult-projectile-find-file` (already a dependency), or
`project-find-file`, or `consult-fd`.

### B3 — All four `consult-projectile` bindings are shadowed (`init.el:373-379`) — **P0**

`:bind` without `:map` binds in the **global** map. But `projectile-mode-map`
binds `C-c p` to `projectile-command-map`, and minor-mode maps outrank the global
map — so `C-c p f` never reaches consult-projectile.

**[verified]**

```
C-c p f -> projectile-find-file          (expected: consult-projectile-find-file)
C-c p p -> projectile-switch-project     (expected: consult-projectile-switch-project)
```

Fix — bind inside the prefix map that wins:

```elisp
(use-package consult-projectile
  :after projectile
  :bind (:map projectile-command-map
              ("f" . consult-projectile-find-file)
              ("p" . consult-projectile-switch-project)
              ("b" . consult-projectile-switch-to-buffer)
              ("d" . consult-projectile-find-dir)))
```

### B4 — `package-selected-packages` is `nil` (`custom.el:7`) — **P0**

**[verified]** `package-selected-packages: nil` after a full load.

`M-x package-autoremove` treats every installed package as orphaned and will
offer to delete all ~60 of them. Contributing cause: `custom.el` is loaded at
`init.el:16`, *before* `(package-initialize)` at line 25, so Custom writes back a
package list computed before packages exist.

Fix: load `custom-file` **at the end** of `init.el`, and either commit an
explicit `package-selected-packages` list or stop tracking `custom.el` in git.

### B5 — `visible-bell` is enabled, not disabled (`init.el:45`) — **P1**

`(setq visible-bell -1)` — `visible-bell` is a boolean and `-1` is non-nil.
**[verified]** `visible-bell = t`. The intent (silence) is achieved only because
`ring-bell-function` is `#'ignore` on the next line. Use `nil` for clarity.

### B6 — `tab-always-indent` is set twice, contradictorily — **P1**

`init.el:79` sets `t`; embark's `:init` at `init.el:289` sets `'complete`, and
runs later. **[verified]** `tab-always-indent = complete`.

Also, `completion-cycle-threshold` and `tab-always-indent` in embark's `:init`
belong with **corfu**, not embark — embark does not read either variable. Move
them and delete the duplicate.

### B7 — `auto-save-dir` is an undeclared free variable (`init.el:39-40`) — **P1**

**[verified]** byte-compiler:

```
init.el:39:7: Warning: assignment to free variable 'auto-save-dir'
init.el:40:17: Warning: reference to free variable 'auto-save-dir'
```

Fix: `defvar` it, use a `let`, or inline `expand-file-name` in the transform.

### B8 — Two eglot variables do not exist in Emacs 30.2 — **P1**

`eglot-code-actions-indications` (`init.el:247`) and `eglot-put-doc-in-buffer`
(`init.el:258`).

**[verified]** Byte-compiler flags both as free variables, and neither appears in
`/usr/share/emacs/30.2/lisp/progmodes/eglot.el.gz`. They are silent no-ops today
— they belong to newer eglot releases.

Fix: install eglot from GNU ELPA (drop `:ensure nil`) to actually get those
features, or remove the two lines.

### B9 — `provide` symbols do not match file names — **P1**

- `init.el:546` — `(provide 'init.el)` should be `(provide 'init)` (or nothing).
- `functions.el:74` — `(provide 'functions.el)` should be `(provide 'functions)`.
- `vars.el` — provides nothing. **[verified]** `(featurep 'vars)` = `nil`.

Once fixed, replace `(load "functions.el" nil t)` with `(require 'functions)` —
idempotent, byte-compilable, and `M-x find-library` works.

---

## 2. Redundancies, dead code, inconsistencies

**Duplicated settings**
- `custom-file` set twice — `init.el:14` and `init.el:48`.
- `scroll-preserve-screen-position` set twice — lines 76 and 82.
- `scroll-conservatively` — `20` at line 77, then `1000` at line 83.
- vertico — `:hook (after-init . vertico-mode)` **and** `:init (vertico-mode)`
  (lines 137-139). Keep `:init`.
- corfu `corfu-preselect` — `'prompt` at line 338, `:first` at line 344.
- corfu `corfu-popupinfo-mode t` in `:custom` (line 335) is redundant with
  `(corfu-popupinfo-mode 1)` in `:init` (line 356). It happens to work (it's a
  global minor mode, so the mode variable is a defcustom), but it reads as a
  settings variable and is not idiomatic.

**Split configuration for the same package**
- haskell — `:hook` in `use-package` (388) plus a separate `add-hook` lambda
  (398-401). Merge into one named function.
- go — `use-package go-mode` (409) plus a separate `add-hook` lambda (413-415).
- projectile — `use-package` block (198) plus a detached `with-eval-after-load`
  (211-213). Use `:config`.
- eglot — `use-package` block plus **two** separate `with-eval-after-load` forms
  plus a bare `setq` (lines 234-272).

**Dead / superseded code**
- `init.el:437-448` — an entire commented-out `markdown-mode` block sitting
  directly above the live one. Delete it; git has the history.
- `init.el:428-433` — commented-out `display-buffer-alist` for `*haskell*`.
- `init.el:319-325` — commented-out `flymake-popon`.
- `rust-mode` **and** `rustic` are both configured with format-on-save
  (405-423). rustic supersedes rust-mode (and depends on it). Keep rustic's
  settings only.
- `haskell-mode :mode ("\\.hs\\'" . haskell-mode)` — already autoloaded.

**Icon families**
`all-the-icons` + `all-the-icons-dired` are configured, while `nerd-icons`,
`nerd-icons-dired` and `nerd-icons-corfu` are **installed but never used**.
doom-modeline 4.x defaults to nerd-icons. Pick one family — nerd-icons is the
maintained one and works in the terminal.

**Orphaned packages** (installed, referenced nowhere in the config):
`apheleia`, `cape`, `nerd-icons-corfu`, `nerd-icons-dired`, `modus-themes`,
`alabaster-themes`, `xterm-color`.
(`nerd-icons`, `popon`, `svg-lib`, `shrink-path`, `elisp-refs`, `autothemer`,
`compat`, `dash`, `s`, `f`, `spinner` are legitimate dependencies.)
`apheleia` and `cape` are worth *adopting* rather than removing — see §4.

**Naming / style**
- `my-eglot-clean-haskell-markdown` (`init.el:261`) breaks the `dk/` convention.
  It also advises `eglot--format-markup` for *all* languages and does a
  `string-match-p` on every doc string; consider gating it on
  `(derived-mode-p 'haskell-mode)`.
- `init.el:535-538` — anonymous lambda on `enable-theme-functions`. It can never
  be removed or redefined without restarting; it also `message`s on every theme
  change. Name it `dk/on-theme-enabled`.
- `keymap.el:11` — `(global-set-key (kbd "S-<return>") (kbd "C-e C-m"))` binds a
  keyboard macro. Use a named command (`dk/open-line-below`) so `C-h k` is
  informative and it survives a `C-e`/`C-m` rebinding.
- `keymap.el:18` — lambda calling `kill-buffer`; `kill-current-buffer` already
  exists.
- `keymap.el` uses `global-set-key`; Emacs 29+ prefers `keymap-global-set` with
  string key descriptions, which validates the key at call time.
- `functions.el:63-72` — `dk/markdown-prose` and `dk/org-prose` are byte-for-byte
  identical. Make one `dk/prose-setup`, or a small `dk/prose-mode` minor mode.
- `functions.el:13` — `add-hook` sits next to the `defun`. Keeping definitions in
  a lib file and *activations* in the owning module makes the load order legible.
- `init.el:44` — `(setq delete-by-moving-to-trash (not noninteractive))`; the
  guard is meaningless in `init.el`. Just `t`.

**Keybinding design notes** (not bugs — record the intent)
- `C-s` → `consult-line` and `C-r` → `consult-history` together remove both
  isearch entry points, yet `init.el:57-60` tunes four isearch variables that can
  now only be reached via `M-s .` etc. Either keep isearch on a key or drop the
  tuning. Also, `consult-history` is designed for minibuffer/comint keymaps —
  globally it will error in an ordinary buffer.
- `M-l` is globally `downcase-dwim` (`keymap.el:14`) and `corfu-show-location`
  inside `corfu-map`. No actual conflict; noted for the record.

---

## 3. Refactoring proposal — structure

Current: `init.el` 547 lines, monolithic, with sections separated by `;;;;` bars.
`src/` holds 118 lines across three files, one of which (`vars.el`) is empty.
`early-init.el` contains two list-helper functions and nothing else.

### 3.1 Target layout

```
early-init.el            ; GC, frame suppression, package-quickstart, native-comp
init.el                  ; ~40 lines: bootstrap + module list
src/dk-lib.el            ; path helpers (moved out of early-init.el)
src/dk-core.el           ; backups, autosave, history, sane defaults
src/dk-ui.el             ; fonts, modeline, line numbers, hl-line, scrolling
src/dk-theme.el          ; theme load + dk/on-theme-enabled
src/dk-keys.el           ; global keymap (was keymap.el)
src/dk-funcs.el          ; shared helpers (was functions.el)
src/dk-completion.el     ; vertico/orderless/marginalia/corfu/cape/embark/consult
src/dk-editing.el        ; expand-region, electric-pair, delete-selection, ws
src/dk-project.el        ; projectile, consult-projectile, dired, magit
src/dk-lsp.el            ; eglot, flymake, eldoc, treesit
src/dk-lang-c.el
src/dk-lang-rust.el
src/dk-lang-go.el
src/dk-lang-haskell.el
src/dk-lang-elisp.el
src/dk-org.el
src/dk-markdown.el
```

Prefix every file `dk-` so `M-x find-library dk-<TAB>` enumerates your own config
and nothing else. Each file ends with `(provide 'dk-<name>)` matching the
filename.

### 3.2 Loader with error isolation

Today a single error mid-`init.el` leaves everything below it unconfigured, with
no clear indication of where it stopped. Use:

```elisp
(defconst dk/modules
  '(dk-lib dk-core dk-ui dk-theme dk-funcs dk-keys
    dk-completion dk-editing dk-project dk-lsp
    dk-lang-c dk-lang-rust dk-lang-go dk-lang-haskell dk-lang-elisp
    dk-org dk-markdown)
  "Config modules, loaded in order.")

(defvar dk/module-errors nil)

(dolist (m dk/modules)
  (condition-case err
      (require m)
    (error
     (push (cons m err) dk/module-errors)
     (message "[config] %s failed: %s" m (error-message-string err)))))
```

One broken module then costs you that module, not the whole session, and
`dk/module-errors` tells you exactly what to look at.

### 3.3 Simplify the path helpers

`early-init.el` currently defines `dk/add-to-confdir` and `dk/add-paths-to-list`
— 10 lines to do what a built-in already does, and `early-init.el` is the wrong
home for them (it exists for things that must run *before* the first frame).
`locate-user-emacs-file` is already used at `init.el:14`:

```elisp
(add-to-list 'load-path (locate-user-emacs-file "src"))
(add-to-list 'custom-theme-load-path (locate-user-emacs-file "themes"))
```

If you keep the helpers for other uses, move them to `src/dk-lib.el`.

### 3.4 What `early-init.el` should actually contain

`init.el:65-69` disables the scroll bar, tool bar and menu bar **after** the
frame is created — that is a visible flicker plus a wasted frame resize on every
start. Suppress them before the frame exists:

```elisp
;;; early-init.el -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-

;; Defer GC through startup, restore a sane threshold afterwards.
(setq gc-cons-threshold most-positive-fixnum
      gc-cons-percentage 0.6)
(add-hook 'emacs-startup-hook
          (lambda ()
            (setq gc-cons-threshold (* 64 1024 1024)
                  gc-cons-percentage 0.1)))

(setq load-prefer-newer t)              ; never load a stale .elc
(setq site-run-file nil)
(setq frame-inhibit-implied-resize t)   ; no resize per font/UI change
(setq inhibit-startup-message t
      inhibit-startup-echo-area-message user-login-name)
(setq native-comp-async-report-warnings-errors 'silent)
(setq package-quickstart t)             ; big win with ~60 packages

;; No flicker: never create these, rather than creating and removing them.
(push '(menu-bar-lines . 0)   default-frame-alist)
(push '(tool-bar-lines . 0)   default-frame-alist)
(push '(vertical-scroll-bars) default-frame-alist)
```

Then delete `scroll-bar-mode`/`tool-bar-mode`/`menu-bar-mode` and the
`gc-cons-threshold` line from `init.el`.

Note the current permanent `(setq gc-cons-threshold (* 50 1000 1000))`
(`init.el:54`) is a defensible choice, but it means every GC scans up to 50 MB of
garbage at once — occasional multi-hundred-ms pauses. The
`most-positive-fixnum`-then-restore pattern above gets the startup benefit
without the steady-state cost. `gcmh` automates the same idea if you prefer.

---

## 4. What to add (best practices)

### 4.1 The one big gap: version control

**`magit` is not installed at all.** For a config that supports Rust, Go,
Haskell, C and Markdown, this is the single largest omission. Add:

```elisp
(use-package magit
  :bind (("C-x g" . magit-status)
         ("C-c g" . magit-file-dispatch))
  :custom (magit-diff-refine-hunk 'all))

(use-package diff-hl                       ; fringe git indicators
  :hook ((prog-mode . diff-hl-mode)
         (dired-mode . diff-hl-dired-mode)
         (magit-post-refresh . diff-hl-magit-post-refresh)))
```

### 4.2 Tree-sitter is installed but never wired up — **P1**

`tree-sitter/` contains 9 compiled grammars:

```
bash  c  cmake  cpp  json  python  rust  toml  yaml
```

but nothing in the config sets `major-mode-remap-alist`, so `c-ts-mode`,
`c++-ts-mode` and `rust-ts-mode` **never activate**. Consequences:

- The eglot hooks on `c-ts-mode` / `c++-ts-mode` (`init.el:237-239`) are
  unreachable — only the `c-mode` / `c++-mode` hooks fire.
- The grammars are dead weight.

Add:

```elisp
(setq treesit-font-lock-level 4)
(dolist (m '((c-mode      . c-ts-mode)
             (c++-mode    . c++-ts-mode)
             (rust-mode   . rust-ts-mode)
             (json-mode   . json-ts-mode)
             (python-mode . python-ts-mode)
             (sh-mode     . bash-ts-mode)
             (yaml-mode   . yaml-ts-mode)
             (toml-mode   . toml-ts-mode)))
  (add-to-list 'major-mode-remap-alist m))
```

Or use `treesit-auto`, which also handles grammar installation. Note: remapping
`rust-mode` → `rust-ts-mode` interacts with rustic — decide which owns `.rs` and
configure only that one. Also consider adding `go` and `gomod` grammars.

### 4.3 LSP throughput — **P1**

`read-process-output-max` is **not set** anywhere. The default is 4096 bytes,
which throttles eglot noticeably on large `rust-analyzer` / HLS responses:

```elisp
(setq read-process-output-max (* 1024 1024))
(setq process-adaptive-read-buffering nil)
```

Also worth having, given the existing eglot setup:

```elisp
(setq eglot-autoshutdown t
      eglot-sync-connect nil          ; don't block on server startup
      eglot-confirm-server-edits nil)
```

### 4.4 Adopt the two packages already installed but unused

**`apheleia`** — async format-on-save. It would replace **four** different
mechanisms currently in play: `rust-format-on-save` (405), `rustic-format-on-save`
(423), `gofmt-before-save` (413-415), and `dk/clang-fos`'s `before-save-hook`
(functions.el:58-60). Apheleia formats in a subprocess and applies an RCS patch,
so it preserves point/markers and does not block the UI or race with LSP edits:

```elisp
(use-package apheleia
  :init (apheleia-global-mode +1))
```

**`cape`** — corfu currently has nothing to complete outside LSP buffers:

```elisp
(use-package cape
  :init
  (add-hook 'completion-at-point-functions #'cape-file)
  (add-hook 'completion-at-point-functions #'cape-dabbrev)
  :custom (cape-dabbrev-min-length 3))
```

### 4.5 File hygiene: `no-littering`

The config root currently holds runtime state: `history`, `places`, `recentf`,
`projectile-bookmarks.eld`, `projectile-frecency.eld`, `autosave/`,
`auto-save-list/`, `.cache/`, `eln-cache/`, plus stray `#!home!...#` autosave
files. `.gitignore` has 20 lines mostly devoted to enumerating them.

```elisp
(use-package no-littering
  :init
  (require 'no-littering)
  (no-littering-theme-backups))
```

Everything then lands in `var/` and `etc/`, and `.gitignore` collapses to a
couple of lines. Load it **early**, before anything that computes a state path
(recentf, savehist, save-place, projectile).

### 4.6 Built-in modes worth enabling (zero cost, Emacs 30)

None of these are currently set:

```elisp
(setq use-short-answers t)              ; replaces the fset at init.el:36
(global-auto-revert-mode 1)             ; essential with external formatters/git
(setq global-auto-revert-non-file-buffers t)
(repeat-mode 1)                         ; C-x o o o, C-x { { {, ...
(winner-mode 1)                         ; C-c <left> undo window layout
(global-so-long-mode 1)                 ; no freeze on minified/one-line files
(context-menu-mode 1)                   ; you already enable xterm-mouse-mode
(setq bookmark-save-flag 1)
(setq history-delete-duplicates t)
(setq savehist-additional-variables
      '(kill-ring search-ring regexp-search-ring))
(setq-default indent-tabs-mode nil)
(setq require-final-newline t)
(setq sentence-end-double-space nil)
(setq completion-ignore-case t
      read-buffer-completion-ignore-case t
      read-file-name-completion-ignore-case t)
```

`use-short-answers` is the Emacs 28+ replacement for
`(fset 'yes-or-no-p 'y-or-n-p)` at `init.el:36` — the `fset` also breaks packages
that legitimately need the long form for destructive prompts.

### 4.7 Other packages that fit the existing stack

- **`vundo`** + **`undo-fu-session`** — you tuned `undo-limit`/`undo-strong-limit`/
  `undo-outer-limit` (51-53) but have no visual undo tree and no undo across
  restarts. `vundo` is tiny and uses the native undo system.
- **`wgrep`** — edit `consult-grep` / `embark-export` results in place and write
  them back. The natural completion of the consult+embark stack you already have.
- **`envrc`** (or `direnv`) — per-project toolchains. Right now
  `haskell-language-server-wrapper` and `rust-analyzer` resolve against the
  global `PATH`; with GHC/rustup version pinning per project that breaks.
- **`consult-eglot`** — workspace symbol search via consult.
- **`consult-dir`** — fast directory jumping in file prompts.
- **`ws-butler`** — trims trailing whitespace only on lines you touched, so diffs
  stay clean without whole-file churn.
- **`eldoc-box`** — childframe hover docs; a better answer to the problem
  `eglot-put-doc-in-buffer` (B8) was reaching for.
- **`exec-path-from-shell`** — only if you ever launch Emacs from a desktop
  launcher rather than a shell.

### 4.8 Window management

`dk/helpful-open` (`functions.el:27-38`) hand-rolls window splitting. The modern
mechanism is `display-buffer-alist`, which is declarative, composable, and would
also cover the commented-out `*haskell*` side-window block at `init.el:428-433`
and flymake/compilation buffers:

```elisp
(add-to-list 'display-buffer-alist
             '("\\*helpful"
               (display-buffer-reuse-window display-buffer-in-side-window)
               (side . right) (window-width . 0.4)))
```

Also, `dk/helpful-close` kills the buffer after `delete-window`; `quit-window`
with a non-nil KILL argument does both and handles the one-window case for you.

### 4.9 Startup performance

- `package-quickstart t` in `early-init.el` (§3.4) — the biggest single win at
  ~60 packages; it collapses all autoload files into one preloaded file.
- `(setq use-package-always-defer t)` plus explicit `:demand` where needed.
  Currently `all-the-icons`, `expand-region`, `doom-themes`, `ef-themes`,
  `gruvbox-theme` and `spacemacs-theme` are all loaded eagerly at every startup
  for no benefit — themes in particular only need to be on `load-path`.
- Byte-compile the config:
  `emacs -Q --batch -L src -f batch-byte-compile src/*.el init.el`
  (with `load-prefer-newer t` set in `early-init.el` so stale `.elc` never wins).
- While tuning: `(setq use-package-compute-statistics t)`, then
  `M-x use-package-report`.

---

## 5. Repository hygiene

**README.md is one line** (`# Vanilla Emacs config`). Worth documenting:
- the module layout and load order
- a keybinding table (the non-obvious ones: `M-x` → `dk/M-x-dwim`, `C-=`,
  `S-<return>`, `C-c k`, `C-c r`, `C-c t`, `S-<backspace>` in the minibuffer)
- required external tools: `haskell-language-server-wrapper`, `rust-analyzer`,
  `clangd`, `gopls`, `pandoc`, `clang-format`, and the **Aporetic Sans Mono**
  font

**No lint or smoke test.** A three-line `Makefile` would catch B1/B7/B8-class
problems before they reach a running Emacs:

```make
compile: ; emacs -Q --batch -L src -f batch-byte-compile src/*.el init.el
smoke:   ; emacs -Q --batch -l early-init.el -l init.el --eval '(kill-emacs 0)'
clean:   ; rm -f src/*.elc *.elc
```

**Font is hard-coded** (`init.el:87-88`). On a machine without Aporetic Sans Mono
you get a silent fallback:

```elisp
(when (find-font (font-spec :name "Aporetic Sans Mono"))
  (set-face-attribute 'default nil :family "Aporetic Sans Mono" :height 115))
```

**32 vendored themes are tracked in git**, exactly one of which
(`gruber-darker`) is loaded — while `doom-themes`, `ef-themes`, `gruvbox-theme`,
`spacemacs-theme` and `modus-themes` are *also* installed from ELPA. Consider
pruning `themes/` to the handful you actually cycle through with `C-c t`.

**`.gitignore` gaps**: `*.elc`, `autosave/`, `projectile.cache` (the path set at
`init.el:202`), and `var/` + `etc/` if you adopt no-littering.

---

## 6. Suggested order of work

1. **P0 correctness** — B1 (eglot servers), B2 (`C-x C-p`), B3 (consult-projectile
   keys), B4 (`package-selected-packages`). Small, isolated, immediately useful.
2. **P1 cleanup** — B5-B9, remove the duplicated settings in §2, delete the
   commented-out blocks. Still no restructuring; the diff stays reviewable.
3. **P2 structure** — move UI suppression into `early-init.el` (§3.4), then split
   `init.el` into `src/dk-*.el` (§3.1) with the isolating loader (§3.2). Do this
   as its own commit with no behaviour changes, so a bisect can separate
   "moved code" from "changed code".
4. **P1/P3 capability** — tree-sitter wiring (§4.2), `read-process-output-max`
   (§4.3), then magit, no-littering, apheleia, cape, and the built-in modes.
5. **P4 polish** — README, Makefile, theme pruning, `.gitignore`.

Steps 1 and 2 are worth doing before 3: fixing bugs inside a file you are about
to split makes the split diff harder to read.
